# Code owners.
#
# Added ahead of the branch-protection rule that will use it. Requiring a
# CODEOWNERS review while this repository has one contributor would freeze
# main entirely — nobody else can approve — so that rule stays off until
# there is a second reviewer. The file exists now so switching it on later
# is one setting rather than another round of decisions.
#
# The paths below are the ones where a wrong change is expensive and quiet:
# infrastructure that holds the data plane open, the pipeline that deploys
# with an OIDC token, the policy that gates it, and the audit primitives.

*                                   @aihebest

# Infrastructure and pipeline: a change here can open a network path, widen a
# role assignment, or alter what deploys, and none of those show up in the
# application's tests.
/infra/                             @aihebest
/.github/workflows/                 @aihebest
/policy/                            @aihebest
/scripts/                           @aihebest

# Audit primitives. AuditEvent.ComputeHash defines what the tamper-evidence
# actually covers — a field quietly dropped from that hash is undetectable by
# design, which is exactly how Reason and OnBehalfOfUserId went uncovered
# until AuditChainVerification was written. See docs/12-Decision-Log.md.
/src/Desicon.Workflow.Domain/Audit/  @aihebest

# Workflow definitions are configuration with the force of code: they decide
# who may approve what, and what the SLA is.
/modules/                           @aihebest
